function injil_help_starter_pages() {
	$section = 'page-section';

	$pages = array(
		// Pillar and method.
		array( 'path' => 'the-islamic-dilemma', 'title' => 'The Islamic Dilemma, Answered', 'template' => $section, 'pattern' => 'islamic-dilemma-page', 'excerpt' => 'The Islamic Dilemma, answered: what the argument claims, why it fails, and the strongest Christian reply.' ),
		array( 'path' => 'method', 'title' => 'The measure', 'pattern' => 'criterion-statement', 'excerpt' => 'The criterion this website uses to weigh every claim.' ),
		array( 'path' => 'corrections', 'title' => 'Corrections log', 'pattern' => 'corrections-log', 'excerpt' => 'Every error found, what the page said, and what it says now.' ),
		array( 'path' => 'answers', 'title' => 'FAQs', 'template' => $section, 'pattern' => 'answers-page', 'excerpt' => 'The questions people search for most, answered and labelled.' ),

		// The Four Gospels.
		array( 'path' => 'gospels', 'title' => 'The Four Gospels', 'template' => $section, 'pattern' => 'section-gospels', 'excerpt' => 'Mark, Matthew, Luke and John examined: authorship, sources, dates and discrepancies.' ),
		array(
			'path' => 'gospels/mark', 'title' => 'Mark, the first gospel', 'excerpt' => 'Anonymous, written around 70 CE, and used by both Matthew and Luke.',
			'body' => array(
				'@consensus',
				'Mark is the shortest gospel and, on the dominant scholarly view, the first written. The text names no author. The name “Mark” comes from church tradition, first recorded by Papias of Hierapolis in the early second century and preserved by Eusebius (Ecclesiastical History 3.39).',
				'## Date and setting',
				'Most scholars place Mark between 65 and 75 CE, close to the Jewish War and the destruction of the Temple in 70 CE. The Greek is plain and the narrative moves quickly: no birth story, no resurrection appearance in the earliest manuscripts.',
				'## Where it ends',
				'The two oldest complete Greek Bibles, Codex Sinaiticus and Codex Vaticanus, both of the fourth century, end the gospel at 16:8 with the women fleeing the empty tomb. Verses 16:9-20 appear in later manuscripts and differ in style and vocabulary. Most modern translations mark them as a later addition.',
				'## Why it matters here',
				'Matthew and Luke both rework Mark. Anything they add or change is therefore visible, and the changes tell us how the early community handled its source. See <a href="/gospels/synoptic-problem/">the synoptic problem</a>.',
				'## Sources',
				'- Bruce Metzger, <em>A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament</em>, notes on Mark 16:9-20.',
				'- Raymond Brown, <em>An Introduction to the New Testament</em>, chapter on Mark.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'gospels/matthew', 'title' => 'Matthew and the law', 'excerpt' => 'Mark rewritten for a Jewish-Christian community: a genealogy, a nativity, and a law that does not pass away.',
			'body' => array(
				'@consensus',
				'Matthew reproduces about ninety percent of Mark, adds a large body of teaching shared with Luke (the hypothetical source Q), and supplies material found only here. It was written in Greek, not translated from Hebrew, and is usually dated between 80 and 90 CE.',
				'## The genealogy and the nativity',
				'The gospel opens with a genealogy of Jesus through Joseph (1:1-17), then a nativity centred on Joseph: the dream, the magi, the flight to Egypt. Luke tells a different story, centred on Mary. The two accounts agree only that Jesus was born in Bethlehem of a virgin named Mary, in the days of Herod.',
				'## The law',
				'Matthew 5:17-19 has Jesus say that he has not come to abolish the law, and that not one stroke of it will pass until heaven and earth pass away. No other gospel contains the saying. It matters for the question of Paul, who writes that Christ is the end of the law (Romans 10:4).',
				'## Sources',
				'- Raymond Brown, <em>An Introduction to the New Testament</em>.',
				'- Ulrich Luz, <em>Matthew 1-7: A Commentary</em>.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'gospels/luke', 'title' => 'Luke and his sources', 'excerpt' => 'A preface that admits earlier accounts, a second nativity, and a sequel in Acts.',
			'body' => array(
				'@consensus',
				'Luke is the only gospel to open with a preface in the manner of Greek historians (1:1-4). It says that many had already undertaken to compile an account, that the author followed things closely from the beginning, and that he writes an orderly account for Theophilus.',
				'## What the preface admits',
				'The author is not an eyewitness. He received the tradition from those who were, and he drew on earlier written accounts. That agrees with the synoptic evidence: Luke uses Mark and the sayings source shared with Matthew.',
				'## Luke and Acts',
				'Luke and Acts are one work in two volumes by the same author, written around 80 to 90 CE. Acts is the only narrative of Paul’s career apart from his own letters, and the two do not always agree. See <a href="/paul/acts-galatians/">Acts against Galatians</a>.',
				'## The genealogy',
				'Luke traces Jesus through Joseph back to Adam (3:23-38). The list differs from Matthew’s in almost every name between David and Joseph, including the name of Joseph’s father (Heli in Luke, Jacob in Matthew).',
				'## Sources',
				'- Joseph Fitzmyer, <em>The Gospel According to Luke</em>, Anchor Bible.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'gospels/john', 'title' => 'John and the Jesus who speaks of himself', 'excerpt' => 'The latest gospel, and the only one in which Jesus makes plain claims about his own nature.',
			'body' => array(
				'@consensus',
				'John is usually dated between 90 and 100 CE and differs from the other three in structure, vocabulary and theology. It has no birth narrative, no exorcisms, and no parables. Jesus speaks at length about himself: the “I am” sayings, the discourses, and the prayer of chapter 17.',
				'## The prologue',
				'John 1:1-18 identifies the Word (Logos) who was with God and was God, and who became flesh. This is the clearest statement of pre-existence in the four gospels. It is not found in Mark, which is the earliest.',
				'## Two voices in one gospel',
				'John also preserves sayings that point the other way: “the Father is greater than I” (14:28) and “this is eternal life, that they know you, the only true God” (17:3). Christian tradition reads these as speaking of the Son’s role. The Islamic reading takes them at face value.',
				'## Sources',
				'- Raymond Brown, <em>The Gospel According to John</em>, Anchor Bible.',
				'- Raymond Brown, <em>An Introduction to the New Testament</em>.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'gospels/thomas', 'title' => 'The Gospel of Thomas', 'excerpt' => 'A sayings collection with no narrative: what it is, when it was written, and where its sayings are used in this gospel.',
			'body' => array(
				'@consensus',
				'The Gospel of Thomas is a collection of 114 sayings attributed to Jesus. It has no birth story, no miracles, no arrest, no crucifixion and no resurrection. Its opening line names the writer as Didymos Judas Thomas, and the name is a claim of authority, not evidence of authorship.',
				'## Manuscripts',
				'The text survives complete only in Coptic, in a codex found at Nag Hammadi in Egypt in 1945 and copied in the fourth century. Three Greek fragments from Oxyrhynchus, copied around 200 CE, show that the work is older than the Coptic copy.',
				'## Date and sources',
				'Scholars date the work anywhere from the late first century to the middle of the second, and they divide on whether it depends on the canonical gospels or preserves independent tradition. About half its sayings have a parallel in Matthew, Mark or Luke. The rest are unique to Thomas, and many carry an ascetic and mystical tone that the Qur’an does not share.',
				'@minority',
				'A minority of scholars hold that Thomas preserves sayings older than the canonical gospels. Others, such as Nicholas Perrin, argue that it was composed from them and from a harmony of the four, the Diatessaron.',
				'## How it is used here',
				'The sayings of Thomas are not set out on a page of their own. Those that agree with the Qur’an as the measure are woven into the chapters of <a href="/gospel-according-to-islam/">The Gospel According to Islam</a>, each marked with its saying number. A saying that the measure does not accept, such as the closing saying about Mary (114), is left out.',
				'## Sources',
				'- Thomas O. Lambdin (trans.), “The Gospel of Thomas”, in <em>The Nag Hammadi Library in English</em>, ed. James M. Robinson, 3rd ed. (Leiden: E. J. Brill, 1988). Saying numbers here follow this edition; the wording used in the chapters is our own digest of it.',
				'- Helmut Koester, <em>Ancient Christian Gospels</em>.',
				'- Nicholas Perrin, <em>Thomas and Tatian</em>.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'gospels/synoptic-problem', 'title' => 'The synoptic problem', 'excerpt' => 'Why Matthew, Mark and Luke share whole sentences, and what Markan priority and Q explain.',
			'body' => array(
				'@consensus',
				'Matthew, Mark and Luke agree in order, wording and even in rare Greek words, far beyond what independent accounts of the same events would produce. Some literary relationship exists. The question of which way it runs is the synoptic problem.',
				'## Markan priority',
				'Most scholars hold that Mark was written first and that Matthew and Luke each used it. Matthew and Luke seldom agree against Mark in order, and Mark’s rougher Greek is more easily explained as the original than as a simplification.',
				'## Q',
				'About 230 verses are shared by Matthew and Luke but absent from Mark, chiefly sayings. The usual explanation is a second source, called Q (German <em>Quelle</em>, “source”). No copy of Q survives.',
				'@minority',
				'A minority of scholars reject Q. The Farrer hypothesis holds that Luke used Matthew as well as Mark. The older Griesbach hypothesis holds that Mark abbreviated Matthew and Luke. Both remove the need for Q.',
				'## Why it matters here',
				'On any of these solutions the gospels are not independent eyewitness reports. They are edited works that depend on earlier written sources.',
				'## Sources',
				'- Mark Goodacre, <em>The Synoptic Problem: A Way Through the Maze</em>.',
				'- Bart Ehrman, <em>The New Testament: A Historical Introduction to the Early Christian Writings</em>.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'gospels/discrepancies', 'title' => 'The discrepancy tables', 'excerpt' => 'Genealogies, nativity, Last Supper, crucifixion and resurrection, set side by side with references.',
			'body' => array(
				'Each table sets the four gospels side by side, with chapter and verse, and records only what the texts say. Interpretation is kept apart from the table.',
				'- <a href="/gospels/discrepancies/resurrection/">The resurrection accounts</a>: who goes to the tomb, who is there, what is said, and where Jesus appears.',
				'More tables will be added here as they are completed: the genealogies (Matthew 1 and Luke 3), the nativity, the Last Supper and the crucifixion. Every new table is announced in the <a href="/corrections/">corrections log</a> if it changes an existing page.',
			),
		),
		array( 'path' => 'gospels/discrepancies/resurrection', 'title' => 'The resurrection accounts compared', 'template' => 'page-wide', 'pattern' => 'discrepancy-resurrection', 'excerpt' => 'The four resurrection accounts set side by side, with references.' ),

		// Paul.
		array( 'path' => 'paul', 'title' => 'Paul of Tarsus', 'template' => $section, 'pattern' => 'section-paul', 'excerpt' => 'Paul against the men who knew ʿĪsā ibn Maryam: his letters, the account in Acts, and the Jerusalem church.' ),
		array(
			'path' => 'paul/acts-galatians', 'title' => 'Acts against Galatians', 'excerpt' => 'Two accounts of the same years that cannot both be right: Damascus, Jerusalem, and the council.',
			'body' => array(
				'@consensus',
				'Paul describes his early career in Galatians 1-2. Luke describes it in Acts 9-15. The two cover the same ground and differ at several points.',
				'## After the conversion',
				'Galatians 1:15-17: Paul says he did not consult anyone but went away to Arabia and then returned to Damascus. Acts 9:19-25 has him spend days with the disciples in Damascus and preach in the synagogues, with no journey to Arabia.',
				'## The first visit to Jerusalem',
				'Galatians 1:18-20: after three years he went up to Jerusalem to visit Cephas, stayed fifteen days, and saw no other apostle except James. Acts 9:26-30: Barnabas brings him to the apostles, and he moves freely among them in Jerusalem.',
				'## The council',
				'Galatians 2:1-10 reports a meeting fourteen years later with Barnabas and Titus, at which the leaders “added nothing” to him. Acts 15 reports a council at which James proposes a decree: gentiles are to abstain from idol meat, blood, strangled animals and sexual immorality. Paul never mentions the decree in his letters, and in 1 Corinthians 8-10 he treats idol meat as a matter of conscience.',
				'## How scholars handle it',
				'Where the two disagree, historians generally prefer Paul’s own letters to the later account in Acts, since Paul writes first-hand and within his own lifetime. This page rests on that rule.',
				'## Sources',
				'- Hans Dieter Betz, <em>Galatians</em>, Hermeneia.',
				'- Joseph Fitzmyer, <em>The Acts of the Apostles</em>, Anchor Bible.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'paul/paul-against-jesus', 'title' => 'Paul against Jesus', 'excerpt' => 'On the law, on sonship, and on atonement, set against the teaching recorded in the gospels.',
			'body' => array(
				'@islamic',
				'The Islamic position, argued here, is that Paul’s teaching departs from the teaching of Jesus recorded in the gospels. Most Christian scholars disagree and hold that Paul faithfully interprets Jesus. Both readings are set out so the reader can judge.',
				'## The law',
				'Matthew 5:17-19: Jesus has not come to abolish the law, and whoever relaxes the least commandment will be called least in the kingdom. Galatians 2:16: no one is justified by works of the law. Romans 10:4: Christ is the end of the law.',
				'## What must one do?',
				'Mark 10:17-19: asked what to do to inherit eternal life, Jesus lists the commandments. Romans 3:28: a person is justified by faith apart from works of the law.',
				'## The Christian reply',
				'Christian interpreters answer that Jesus fulfilled the law and that Paul speaks of the ritual boundary markers (circumcision, food laws) that separated Jew from gentile, not of the moral law. Scholars of the “new perspective” on Paul, such as E. P. Sanders and James Dunn, develop this reading.',
				'## Where this page stands',
				'The Qur’an speaks of Jesus confirming the Torah (3:50; 5:46). On the matter of the law, the Qur’anic portrait is closer to Matthew 5 than to Galatians. That is the argument; the evidence is above.',
				'## Sources',
				'- E. P. Sanders, <em>Paul and Palestinian Judaism</em>.',
				'- James D. G. Dunn, <em>The Theology of Paul the Apostle</em>.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'paul/james', 'title' => 'James and the Jerusalem church', 'excerpt' => 'The brother who led the first community, and what it required of Paul.',
			'body' => array(
				'@consensus',
				'James, called the brother of the Lord, led the Jerusalem community after Jesus. Paul names him among the “pillars” (Galatians 2:9) and the first apostle he met after three years (1:19).',
				'## The men from James',
				'Galatians 2:12 reports that men came “from James” to Antioch, and that Peter withdrew from eating with gentiles. Paul opposed Peter to his face. The episode shows that the Jerusalem leaders did not share Paul’s position on table fellowship.',
				'## Acts 21',
				'Acts 21:18-25 has James and the elders report that thousands of Jews have believed and are all zealous for the law, and that they have heard Paul teaches Jews to forsake Moses. Paul is asked to take part in a purification rite to show that he too lives in observance of the law.',
				'## The death of James',
				'Josephus (Antiquities 20.200) records the stoning of “James, the brother of Jesus who was called Christ” in 62 CE, on the order of the high priest Ananus. Hegesippus, quoted by Eusebius (Ecclesiastical History 2.23), describes James as strictly observant.',
				'## Sources',
				'- Richard Bauckham, <em>Jude and the Relatives of Jesus in the Early Church</em>.',
				'- Josephus, <em>Jewish Antiquities</em>, book 20.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'paul/ebionites', 'title' => 'The Ebionites', 'excerpt' => 'The early Jewish followers of Jesus who kept the law and rejected Paul.',
			'body' => array(
				'@consensus',
				'The Ebionites (from the Hebrew <em>ebyonim</em>, “the poor”) were a Jewish-Christian group known to us chiefly from the church fathers, who opposed them. They are attested from the second century.',
				'## What the fathers report',
				'Irenaeus (Against Heresies 1.26.2) says the Ebionites used only the Gospel of Matthew, rejected Paul as an apostate from the law, practised circumcision and followed Jewish customs. Eusebius (Ecclesiastical History 3.27) says they held Jesus to be a plain man, born of Joseph and Mary.',
				'## What is uncertain',
				'Our sources are hostile and later than the group’s origins. Scholars debate whether the Ebionites descend directly from the Jerusalem church or arose afterwards. No Ebionite writing survives intact.',
				'## Why it matters here',
				'The existence of Jewish followers of Jesus who kept the law and refused Paul shows that his version of the message was contested from the start. See <a href="/paul/james/">James and the Jerusalem church</a>.',
				'## Sources',
				'- Irenaeus, <em>Against Heresies</em>, book 1.',
				'- Eusebius, <em>Ecclesiastical History</em>, book 3.',
				'- Oskar Skarsaune and Reidar Hvalvik (eds.), <em>Jewish Believers in Jesus: The Early Centuries</em>.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'paul/seven-letters', 'title' => 'The seven letters', 'excerpt' => 'Which letters scholars accept as Paul’s own, and why the argument here rests on them alone.',
			'body' => array(
				'@consensus',
				'Thirteen letters carry Paul’s name. Scholars sort them into three groups.',
				'- Undisputed (seven): Romans, 1 Corinthians, 2 Corinthians, Galatians, Philippians, 1 Thessalonians and Philemon.',
				'- Disputed: Ephesians, Colossians and 2 Thessalonians. Scholars divide on authorship.',
				'- Widely held to be later: 1 and 2 Timothy and Titus (the Pastoral Letters).',
				'## The rule used on this website',
				'Where a claim about Paul’s own teaching is made, it rests on the seven undisputed letters. A saying from Ephesians or the Pastorals is not used to show what Paul himself taught. This keeps the argument within ground that critical scholarship broadly accepts.',
				'## Sources',
				'- Raymond Brown, <em>An Introduction to the New Testament</em>.',
				'- Bart Ehrman, <em>Forged: Writing in the Name of God</em>.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'paul/false-apostles', 'title' => 'The false apostles', 'excerpt' => 'Paul’s own charge against his rivals (2 Corinthians 11:13), and who stood on the other side of it.',
			'body' => array(
				'@consensus',
				'In 2 Corinthians 11:13 Paul writes of “false apostles, deceitful workers, disguising themselves as apostles of Christ”. In 11:5 and 12:11 he calls them the “super-apostles”. The rivals are never named.',
				'## Who were they?',
				'Scholars disagree. Some identify them with Jewish-Christian missionaries from Jerusalem, perhaps linked to the “men from James”. Others see itinerant preachers of a different kind. The letter gives only Paul’s side.',
				'## An accusation with two edges',
				'Galatians 1:6-9 curses anyone who preaches a gospel different from his own, and 2:4 speaks of “false brothers”. The charge of falsehood runs in both directions: Paul’s opponents, whose letters are lost, would have said the same of him.',
				'@islamic',
				'The Islamic reading takes the dispute as evidence that the first community was divided over Paul, and that the side which lost the argument did not leave a record. The evidence for that claim is the Ebionite testimony and Acts 21.',
				'## Sources',
				'- Margaret Thrall, <em>The Second Epistle to the Corinthians</em>, International Critical Commentary.',
			),
		),

		// Jesus in the Qur'an.
		array( 'path' => 'quran', 'title' => 'Jesus in the Qur’an', 'template' => $section, 'pattern' => 'section-quran', 'excerpt' => 'What the Qur’an says of ʿĪsā ibn Maryam, passage by passage.' ),
		array(
			'path' => 'quran/birth', 'title' => 'The annunciation and the birth', 'excerpt' => 'The angels and Maryam, the palm tree, and the infant who speaks.',
			'body' => array(
				'@islamic',
				'The Qur’an tells of Maryam in two places. Sūrat Āl ʿImrān (3:42-47) has the angels announce a word from God, whose name is the Messiah, ʿĪsā ibn Maryam. Sūrat Maryam (19:16-34) tells the story in full.',
				'## The announcement',
				'Maryam withdraws to an eastern place. A spirit sent by God appears as a man and announces a pure boy. She asks how, since no man has touched her. The answer is that God creates what He wills (3:47; 19:20-21).',
				'## The birth',
				'The pains of labour bring her to the trunk of a palm tree. She wishes she had died before this. A voice calls to her from beneath: do not grieve, a stream has been placed below you, and the palm will drop ripe dates (19:23-26).',
				'## The infant speaks',
				'She brings the child to her people, who accuse her. She points to him. He says: “I am the servant of God. He has given me the Book and made me a prophet” (19:30). The speech is a defence of Maryam and a statement of what he is.',
				'## Compare',
				'Luke 1-2 and Matthew 1-2 tell of the virgin birth; neither has the palm tree or the infant’s speech. The infant who speaks appears in the Arabic Infancy Gospel, a later text.',
				'## Sources',
				'- Gabriel Said Reynolds, <em>The Qur’ān and Its Biblical Subtext</em>.',
				'- Ṭabarī, <em>Jāmiʿ al-bayān</em>, on 3:42-47 and 19:16-34.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'quran/signs', 'title' => 'The signs', 'excerpt' => 'The bird of clay, the blind and the leper healed, the dead raised, by the permission of God.',
			'body' => array(
				'@islamic',
				'Qur’an 3:49 and 5:110 list the signs given to ʿĪsā. Each is stated to be by the permission of God, and the phrase is repeated for every sign.',
				'- He fashions from clay the shape of a bird and breathes into it, and it becomes a bird.',
				'- He heals the blind from birth and the leper.',
				'- He gives life to the dead.',
				'- He tells people what they eat and what they store in their houses (3:49).',
				'## Compare',
				'The healing of the blind and the lepers, and the raising of the dead, appear in the gospels (Mark 8:22-26; Luke 7:11-17; John 11). The clay birds do not. They appear in the Infancy Gospel of Thomas, a second-century text, where the child Jesus makes sparrows from clay on the Sabbath.',
				'## The refrain',
				'In every case the Qur’an says “by My permission” (5:110) or “by God’s permission” (3:49). The sign is a gift to the messenger, not a power of his own. This is the Qur’anic measure applied to the miracle stories.',
				'## Sources',
				'- Gabriel Said Reynolds, <em>The Qur’ān and Its Biblical Subtext</em>.',
				'- Bart Ehrman, <em>Lost Scriptures</em>, on the Infancy Gospel of Thomas.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'quran/servant', 'title' => 'A servant and a messenger', 'excerpt' => 'What the Qur’an calls him, and what it says he is not.',
			'body' => array(
				'@islamic',
				'The Qur’an names ʿĪsā a servant (<em>ʿabd</em>), a prophet, and a messenger. It also names him the Messiah, a word from God, and a spirit from Him (4:171). It denies that he is the third of three and denies that he is God’s son.',
				'## What he is',
				'- A servant of God: 19:30; 43:59; 4:172.',
				'- A messenger: 3:49; 5:75; 61:6.',
				'- The Messiah, a word from God and a spirit from Him: 4:171.',
				'## What he is not',
				'Those who say that God is the Messiah, son of Maryam, have disbelieved (5:17, 5:72). Those who say that God is the third of three have disbelieved (5:73). Qur’an 4:171 tells the People of the Book not to go beyond bounds in their religion and not to say “three”.',
				'## The final scene',
				'In 5:116-117 God asks ʿĪsā whether he told people to take him and his mother as gods besides God. He replies that he said only what he was commanded: worship God, my Lord and your Lord.',
				'## Sources',
				'- Ṭabarī and Ibn Kathīr, commentary on 4:171 and 5:116-117.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'quran/table', 'title' => 'The table from heaven', 'excerpt' => 'The disciples ask for a sign, and receive it with a warning.',
			'body' => array(
				'@islamic',
				'Sūrat al-Māʾida, “The Table”, takes its name from 5:112-115. The disciples ask whether God can send down a table from heaven. ʿĪsā tells them to fear God. They say they wish to eat from it, to have their hearts at rest, and to bear witness.',
				'## The prayer and the answer',
				'ʿĪsā prays: “O God, our Lord, send down to us a table from heaven to be a festival for the first of us and the last of us, and a sign from You” (5:114). God answers that He will send it down, and that whoever disbelieves after it will receive a punishment such as no one in the worlds has received (5:115).',
				'## Compare',
				'The gospels have the feeding of the multitudes (Mark 6:30-44; 8:1-10) and the Last Supper (Mark 14:22-25). The Qur’an does not say which, if either, it recalls. Commentators have connected the table with each.',
				'## Sources',
				'- Ṭabarī and Ibn Kathīr, commentary on 5:112-115.',
				'- Gabriel Said Reynolds, <em>The Qur’ān and Its Biblical Subtext</em>.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'quran/raised', 'title' => 'The end of his earthly mission', 'excerpt' => 'Neither killed nor crucified, but raised to God.',
			'body' => array(
				'@islamic',
				'Qur’an 4:157-158 says of those who boast that they killed the Messiah, ʿĪsā ibn Maryam, the messenger of God: “They did not kill him, nor did they crucify him, but it was made to appear so to them. … God raised him to Himself.”',
				'## Related verses',
				'- 3:55: “O ʿĪsā, I will take you (<em>mutawaffīka</em>) and raise you to Myself.”',
				'- 5:117: “When You took me (<em>tawaffaytanī</em>), You were the watcher over them.”',
				'- 19:33: “Peace upon me the day I was born, the day I die, and the day I am raised alive.”',
				'## How Muslims have read it',
				'The majority of classical commentators hold that God raised ʿĪsā alive and that someone else was made to look like him. Some early commentators read <em>tawaffā</em> in 3:55 as death, followed by raising. The disagreement concerns the manner, not the denial that he was crucified.',
				'## The historical question',
				'The crucifixion of Jesus under Pontius Pilate is accepted by nearly all historians, including non-Christian ones, as one of the surest facts about him. That is the scholarly consensus and this website states it plainly. The Qur’an’s statement is a different kind of claim, and the two are set side by side here without blending them.',
				'## Sources',
				'- Ṭabarī, <em>Jāmiʿ al-bayān</em>, on 4:157-158.',
				'- Mahmoud Ayoub, “Towards an Islamic Christology II: The Death of Jesus, Reality or Delusion?”, <em>The Muslim World</em> 70 (1980).',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'quran/ahmad', 'title' => 'The good news of Aḥmad', 'excerpt' => 'The messenger he announced.',
			'body' => array(
				'@islamic',
				'Qur’an 61:6 reports that ʿĪsā said to the Children of Israel: “I am the messenger of God to you, confirming what came before me of the Torah and giving good news of a messenger who will come after me, whose name is Aḥmad.”',
				'## The Muslim reading',
				'Muslim scholars have read the verse as foretelling Muḥammad, the name Aḥmad being from the same root as Muḥammad. Ibn Isḥāq, in the earliest biography, connects the promise with the “Paraclete” of John 15:26 (Guillaume, <em>The Life of Muhammad</em>, p. 104).',
				'## The scholarly reading',
				'@consensus',
				'Scholars of the New Testament identify the Paraclete (<em>paraklētos</em>) of John 14-16 with the Holy Spirit, which John 14:26 states directly. Some Muslim writers argue that the original Greek was <em>periklytos</em>, “the praised one”, which would translate Aḥmad. No Greek manuscript has that reading.',
				'## Where this page stands',
				'The verse is the Qur’an’s own witness. The identification with a passage of John rests on a reading the manuscripts do not support. Both points are stated so the reader can weigh them.',
				'## Sources',
				'- Alfred Guillaume (trans.), <em>The Life of Muhammad</em>.',
				'- Raymond Brown, <em>The Gospel According to John XIII-XXI</em>, on the Paraclete.',
			),
		),

		// The Gospel According to Islam.
		array( 'path' => 'gospel-according-to-islam', 'title' => 'The Gospel According to Islam', 'template' => $section, 'pattern' => 'section-gospel', 'excerpt' => 'A gospel retold with the Qur’an as the measure.' ),
		array( 'path' => 'gospel-according-to-islam/how-composed', 'title' => 'How this gospel was composed', 'pattern' => 'how-composed', 'excerpt' => 'The rules used to compose the gospel: what is kept, what is left out, and why.' ),
		array(
			'path' => 'gospel-according-to-islam/1', 'title' => 'The birth', 'excerpt' => 'Maryam in the sanctuary, the announcement, and the infant who speaks.',
			'body' => array(
				'@islamic',
				'Maryam was the daughter of ʿImrān. Her mother had vowed the child to God’s service before she was born, and said, when a girl was born, that God knew best what she had delivered (3:35-36). Zakariyyā became her guardian, and whenever he entered the sanctuary he found provision with her (3:37).',
				'The angels said: “O Maryam, God gives you good news of a word from Him, whose name is the Messiah, ʿĪsā, son of Maryam, honoured in this world and the next, and among those brought near” (3:45). He would speak to people in the cradle and in maturity (3:46).',
				'She withdrew to an eastern place. She came to the trunk of a palm tree, and a voice called from beneath her (19:22-24). She brought the child to her people. She pointed to him, and the infant said: “I am the servant of God. He has given me the Book and made me a prophet” (19:29-30).',
				'## The measure',
				'The Qur’an has no star, no manger, no Bethlehem and no Herod. It has a mother, a miracle and a child who speaks in her defence. The chapter keeps to that.',
				'## From the Gospel of Thomas',
				'The Gospel of Thomas is a collection of sayings and tells nothing of the birth. The stories of the infancy found under the name of Thomas belong to a different and later work, the Infancy Gospel of Thomas.{{fn:Saying numbers follow the standard edition. The wording is our own digest of Thomas O. Lambdin’s translation in <em>The Nag Hammadi Library in English</em>, ed. James M. Robinson (Leiden: E. J. Brill, 1988). See <a href="/gospels/thomas/">The Gospel of Thomas</a>.}}',
				'## Passages',
				'3:35-47; 19:16-34. Compare Matthew 1-2 and Luke 1-2 on <a href="/quran/birth/">the Qur’an page</a>.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'gospel-according-to-islam/2', 'title' => 'The ministry', 'excerpt' => 'John the Baptist, the call, and the first disciples.',
			'body' => array(
				'@islamic',
				'Yaḥyā, whom the gospels call John, is the first witness. Zakariyyā was told of a son named Yaḥyā who would confirm a word from God, and who would be noble, chaste and a prophet among the righteous (3:39). The Qur’an says that God gave him wisdom as a boy (19:12) and that he was dutiful to his parents and not arrogant (19:14).',
				'ʿĪsā calls those who will help him. “Who will be my helpers to God?” he asks, and the disciples (<em>al-ḥawāriyyūn</em>) answer: “We are God’s helpers. We believe in God, and bear witness that we are Muslims” (3:52).',
				'## What the Qur’an leaves out',
				'The Qur’an does not tell of a baptism, a voice from heaven or a temptation in the wilderness. Those scenes belong to the gospels (Mark 1:9-13). This chapter does not supply them. Where the Qur’an is silent, the measure is silence.',
				'## From the Gospel of Thomas',
				'The Gospel of Thomas keeps three sayings that stand beside the Qur’anic portrait of the first helpers and of Yaḥyā:{{fn:Saying numbers follow the standard edition. The wording is our own digest of Thomas O. Lambdin’s translation in <em>The Nag Hammadi Library in English</em>, ed. James M. Robinson (Leiden: E. J. Brill, 1988). See <a href="/gospels/thomas/">The Gospel of Thomas</a>.}}',
				'- <strong>Thomas 46.</strong> No one born of woman is greater than John the Baptist, yet whoever becomes a child will know the kingdom and surpass John. (Compare Matthew 11:11.)',
				'- <strong>Thomas 73.</strong> The harvest is large and the workers are few. Ask the Lord to send workers. (Compare Matthew 9:37-38.)',
				'- <strong>Thomas 99.</strong> Told that his brothers and mother are outside, Jesus says those who do the Father’s will are his brothers and mother. (Compare Mark 3:31-35.)',
				'## Passages',
				'3:39; 3:52-53; 19:12-15; 61:14.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'gospel-according-to-islam/3', 'title' => 'The teaching and the signs', 'excerpt' => 'The law upheld, the signs given by permission of God.',
			'body' => array(
				'@islamic',
				'ʿĪsā came to the Children of Israel with a sign from their Lord (3:49). He said: “I have come confirming the Torah that was before me, and to make lawful for you some of what was forbidden to you” (3:50). The Injīl was given to him “with guidance and light, confirming the Torah” (5:46).',
				'He came with clear proofs and with wisdom (43:63). He told his people to fear God and obey him, for God is his Lord and theirs, and this is a straight path (43:63-64; 3:51).',
				'The signs came by God’s permission: the bird of clay, the healing of the blind and the leper, and the raising of the dead (3:49; 5:110). See <a href="/quran/signs/">the signs</a>.',
				'## The measure',
				'The chapter agrees with Matthew 5:17-19 that the law is upheld, and with 3:50 that some restrictions were eased. It sets aside the claim that the signs belonged to the one who performed them.',
				'## From the Gospel of Thomas',
				'The Gospel of Thomas keeps many sayings of the teacher. These agree with the Qur’an as the measure on honesty, mercy and the service of God. Saying 95, against lending at interest, agrees with Qur’an 2:275.{{fn:Saying numbers follow the standard edition. The wording is our own digest of Thomas O. Lambdin’s translation in <em>The Nag Hammadi Library in English</em>, ed. James M. Robinson (Leiden: E. J. Brill, 1988). See <a href="/gospels/thomas/">The Gospel of Thomas</a>.}}',
				'- <strong>Thomas 9.</strong> The sower’s seed falls on road, rock and thorns, and some on good soil, which yields sixty and a hundredfold. (Compare Mark 4:3-9.)',
				'- <strong>Thomas 20.</strong> The kingdom is like a mustard seed, the smallest of seeds, which grows into a plant sheltering birds. (Compare Mark 4:30-32.)',
				'- <strong>Thomas 26.</strong> You see the speck in your brother’s eye and not the beam in your own. Remove the beam first. (Compare Matthew 7:3-5.)',
				'- <strong>Thomas 31.</strong> No prophet is welcomed in his own village, and no doctor heals those who know him. (Compare Luke 4:24.)',
				'- <strong>Thomas 33.</strong> Preach from the housetops what you hear. No one hides a lamp under a bushel. It goes on a stand for all to see. (Compare Matthew 10:27; 5:15.)',
				'- <strong>Thomas 34.</strong> If a blind man leads a blind man, both fall into a pit. (Compare Matthew 15:14.)',
				'- <strong>Thomas 47.</strong> No one can ride two horses or draw two bows or serve two masters. Old wine does not go into new skins, nor new wine into old. (Compare Luke 16:13; 5:36-39.)',
				'- <strong>Thomas 54.</strong> Blessed are the poor, for the kingdom of heaven is theirs. (Compare Luke 6:20.)',
				'- <strong>Thomas 89.</strong> Why wash the outside of the cup? The one who made the inside made the outside. (Compare Luke 11:39-40.)',
				'- <strong>Thomas 90.</strong> Come to me, for my yoke is easy and my lordship gentle, and you will find rest. (Compare Matthew 11:28-30.)',
				'- <strong>Thomas 92.</strong> Seek and you will find. What you asked before and I did not say, I now want to tell, but you do not ask. (Compare Matthew 7:7.)',
				'- <strong>Thomas 94.</strong> Whoever seeks will find, and to the one who knocks it will be opened. (Compare Matthew 7:8.)',
				'- <strong>Thomas 95.</strong> If you have money, do not lend it at interest. Give it to those from whom you will not get it back. (Compare Luke 6:34-35.)',
				'- <strong>Thomas 96.</strong> The kingdom is like a woman who hides a little leaven in dough and makes large loaves. (Compare Matthew 13:33.)',
				'- <strong>Thomas 107.</strong> The kingdom is like a shepherd whose largest sheep strays. He leaves the ninety-nine to find it, and says he loves it more than the ninety-nine. (Compare Matthew 18:12-13.)',
				'## Passages',
				'3:49-51; 5:46; 5:110; 43:63-64.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'gospel-according-to-islam/4', 'title' => 'Jerusalem', 'excerpt' => 'The Temple, the priests, and the plot.',
			'body' => array(
				'@islamic',
				'The Qur’an has no narrative of ʿĪsā’s last days in Jerusalem. It has no entry, no Temple scene, no trial. It records the plot in outline: when ʿĪsā sensed disbelief among them, he asked who would be his helpers to God (3:52), and “they planned, and God planned, and God is the best of planners” (3:54).',
				'The gospels supply the setting: the entry into Jerusalem, the action in the Temple, the dispute with the chief priests and the plot to arrest him (Mark 11-14). Historians accept the core of this: a Galilean preacher came to Jerusalem at Passover, caused a disturbance in the Temple, and was handed to the Roman governor.',
				'## The measure',
				'The chapter does not retell what the Qur’an does not say. It records the gospel setting in a sentence, marks it as outside the Qur’an, and moves on.',
				'## From the Gospel of Thomas',
				'The Gospel of Thomas has no Jerusalem narrative, but it keeps sayings that belong to these days:{{fn:Saying numbers follow the standard edition. The wording is our own digest of Thomas O. Lambdin’s translation in <em>The Nag Hammadi Library in English</em>, ed. James M. Robinson (Leiden: E. J. Brill, 1988). See <a href="/gospels/thomas/">The Gospel of Thomas</a>.}}',
				'- <strong>Thomas 39.</strong> The Pharisees and scribes have hidden the keys of knowledge and neither enter nor let others enter. Be as wise as serpents and as innocent as doves. (Compare Matthew 23:13; 10:16.)',
				'- <strong>Thomas 65.</strong> A vineyard owner sends servants to collect his share from tenants, who beat them. He sends his son, whom they kill. (Compare Mark 12:1-9.)',
				'- <strong>Thomas 66.</strong> Show me the stone the builders rejected: it is the cornerstone. (Compare Mark 12:10.)',
				'- <strong>Thomas 71.</strong> Jesus will destroy this house and no one will be able to rebuild it. (Compare Mark 14:58.)',
				'- <strong>Thomas 102.</strong> Woe to the Pharisees, who are like a dog sleeping in the oxen’s feed trough, neither eating nor letting the oxen eat.',
				'- <strong>Thomas 104.</strong> Asked to pray and fast, Jesus asks what sin he has committed. When the bridegroom leaves the bridal chamber, then let them fast and pray. (Compare Mark 2:18-20.)',
				'## Passages',
				'3:52-54. Compare Mark 11-14.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'gospel-according-to-islam/5', 'title' => 'The end of his earthly mission', 'excerpt' => 'The plan of his enemies, and the plan of God.',
			'body' => array(
				'@islamic',
				'“They planned, and God planned” (3:54). God said: “O ʿĪsā, I will take you and raise you to Myself and purify you from those who disbelieve, and place those who follow you above those who disbelieve until the Day of Resurrection” (3:55).',
				'The Qur’an says of the boast that they killed the Messiah: “They did not kill him, nor did they crucify him, but it was made to appear so to them” (4:157). “Rather, God raised him to Himself” (4:158).',
				'## What is and is not said',
				'The Qur’an denies the killing and the crucifixion. It does not say who or what appeared in his place, and commentators have offered many answers. This chapter records the denial and the raising, and does not choose among the commentaries. For the historical question, see <a href="/quran/raised/">the Qur’an page</a>.',
				'## From the Gospel of Thomas',
				'The Gospel of Thomas has no account of an arrest, a trial or a crucifixion, so it neither confirms nor contradicts Qur’an 4:157.{{fn:Saying numbers follow the standard edition. The wording is our own digest of Thomas O. Lambdin’s translation in <em>The Nag Hammadi Library in English</em>, ed. James M. Robinson (Leiden: E. J. Brill, 1988). See <a href="/gospels/thomas/">The Gospel of Thomas</a>.}}',
				'## Passages',
				'3:54-55; 4:157-158; 5:117.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'gospel-according-to-islam/6', 'title' => 'The return', 'excerpt' => 'What the Qur’an and the traditions say of his second coming.',
			'body' => array(
				'@islamic',
				'The Qur’an says of ʿĪsā in 43:61: “He is a knowledge of the Hour, so do not doubt it.” Another reading of the same word gives “a sign of the Hour”. Qur’an 4:159 says that none of the People of the Book will fail to believe in him before his death.',
				'Commentators have read both verses as pointing to his return. The traditions are explicit. In the collections of al-Bukhārī and Muslim, the Prophet is reported to have said that the son of Maryam will descend as a just ruler, break the cross and kill the swine.',
				'## What is and is not said',
				'The Qur’an gives the sign, the traditions give the detail. This chapter states the Qur’anic text first and marks the traditions as a separate source, so the reader can see where each begins.',
				'## From the Gospel of Thomas',
				'The Gospel of Thomas keeps two sayings on watching and on the kingdom:{{fn:Saying numbers follow the standard edition. The wording is our own digest of Thomas O. Lambdin’s translation in <em>The Nag Hammadi Library in English</em>, ed. James M. Robinson (Leiden: E. J. Brill, 1988). See <a href="/gospels/thomas/">The Gospel of Thomas</a>.}}',
				'- <strong>Thomas 103.</strong> Fortunate is the one who knows where the robbers will enter and can arm himself before they do. (Compare Matthew 24:43.)',
				'- <strong>Thomas 113.</strong> The disciples ask when the kingdom will come. It will not come by watching for it. It is spread over the earth and people do not see it. (Compare Luke 17:20-21.)',
				'## Passages',
				'4:159; 43:57-61. Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī, Book of the Prophets, on the descent of the son of Maryam; Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim, Book of Faith.',
			),
		),

		array(
			'path' => 'bonus', 'title' => 'Bonus chapters', 'excerpt' => 'Extra chapters that stand beside The Gospel According to Islam.',
			'body' => array(
				'Chapters that stand beside <a href="/gospel-according-to-islam/">The Gospel According to Islam</a> but are not part of its main telling.',
				'- <a href="/bonus/epistle/">An Epistle unto the Congregation of Paul</a>: a letter to the followers of Paul, in the manner of the Pauline epistles.',
			),
		),
		array(
			'path' => 'bonus/epistle', 'title' => 'An Epistle unto the Congregation of Paul', 'excerpt' => 'A letter to the followers of Paul, in the manner of the Pauline epistles.',
			'body' => array(
				'@islamic',
				'I, a bondservant set free from the shadow of Paul{{fn:The opening follows Paul’s own: “Paul, a servant of Jesus Christ” (Romans 1:1; Philippians 1:1; Titus 1:1). The author sets the form of the Pauline letter against Paul.}}, who aforetime walked in the darkness of a false revelation and am now returned unto the doctrine undefiled, write unto thee that yet holdest fast to every gospel that hath been bent from its true course: grace and peace be unto thee from God, Most High and Glorious, and from ʿĪsā al-Masīḥ, His servant, whom He hath held in honour.{{fn:“Grace and peace” is Paul’s greeting formula (Romans 1:7; 1 Corinthians 1:3; Galatians 1:3). Here “servant” (<em>ʿabd</em>) is the Qur’anic title: 4:172; 19:30; 43:59.}}',
				'I give thanks unto God, Most High and Glorious, who in His bounty{{fn:Paul’s letters open with a thanksgiving after the greeting (Romans 1:8; 1 Corinthians 1:4; Philippians 1:3).}} hath opened the eyes of some among His servants, and loosed them from the fetters of a faith inherited without understanding. Wherefore I write, not with the pen of malice, but with the ink of love, born of the light of truth that hath visited the heart and stirred the soul.',
				'Thou hast heard how I, in time past, did defend Paul with all my zeal, reading out his letters{{fn:Paul directs that his letters be read aloud to the congregation (Colossians 4:16; 1 Thessalonians 5:27).}} and guarding him against every reproach. Yet when I was tried with proofs and with light, it was made plain unto me that the gospel I had defended was not the Gospel of ʿĪsā al-Masīḥ, but another gospel: not from heaven, but from the tongue of one who professed to have seen a light upon the road{{fn:The Damascus road: Acts 9:3; 22:6; 26:13. Paul’s own letters mention a revelation but give no account of the road (Galatians 1:11-12, 15-16).}}, with no witness, no confirmation, and no testimony of the congregation{{fn:Acts 9:7 says his companions heard the voice and saw no one. Acts 22:9 says they saw the light but did not hear the voice. Paul himself says he did not confer with anyone for three years (Galatians 1:16-18).}}, and who thereupon named himself an apostle of Christ Jesus.',
				'Hast thou forgotten that Paul never met ʿĪsā al-Masīḥ?{{fn:Paul does not claim to have known Jesus in the flesh. He claims to have seen the risen Lord (1 Corinthians 9:1; 15:8) and to have received his gospel “by the revelation of Jesus Christ” (Galatians 1:11-12). Compare 2 Corinthians 5:16.}} That he never sat to hear His sayings? And knowest thou not that Peter, James, yea, Barnabas himself, did rebuke him and withdrew from his way?{{fn:<strong>Editorial note.</strong> The canonical texts record the reverse in the case of Peter: Paul rebuked Peter at Antioch after “certain came from James”, and even Barnabas was carried away (Galatians 2:11-14). Acts records a “sharp contention” in which Paul and Barnabas parted company (Acts 15:36-40). The elders at Jerusalem under James pressed Paul to show that he kept the law (Acts 21:18-24). A rebuke of Paul by Peter is not recorded in these sources. See <a href="/paul/james/">James and the Jerusalem church</a>.}} How then art thou turned so swiftly from the teaching of ʿĪsā al-Masīḥ, who never laid claim to godhead for Himself{{fn:Mark 10:18; John 14:28; 17:3. In the Qur’an, 5:116-117 and 4:171-172.}}, unto one who maketh Him a god upon feeble reasoning and false speech?',
				'Try every spirit.{{fn:“Beloved, believe not every spirit, but try the spirits whether they are of God” (1 John 4:1, Authorised Version). Compare “Prove all things” (1 Thessalonians 5:21).}} Weigh each saying, and examine every claim. Be not beguiled by the glitter of sweet words that lead astray, for even the adversary doth disguise himself as an angel of light{{fn:2 Corinthians 11:14, where Paul applies the saying to his own rivals (11:13).}}. And is it not the disguised one who cometh bearing the gospel of Christ Jesus, which heaven hath never confirmed?',
				'Knowest thou not that the tale of the Damascus event, as he told it, is at variance with itself?{{fn:Acts 9:3-9, 22:6-11 and 26:12-18 give three tellings. In 9:7 the companions stand speechless; in 26:14 they all fall to the ground. In 9:7 they hear the voice; in 22:9 they do not. See <a href="/paul/acts-galatians/">Acts against Galatians</a>.}} What then is this? A revelation, or his own contriving? If it came from heaven, would not its course be steadfast? If it were truly from God, Most High and Glorious, would it not sound one note? Or is the holy word mingled with confused speech, until the whole world is bewildered who is in the right and who is corrupt?',
				'I bear witness with all my soul and understanding that the gospel which setteth aside the law, putteth away circumcision{{fn:Galatians 5:2-6; 6:15; 1 Corinthians 7:19. Compare Acts 16:3, where Paul has Timothy circumcised, and Genesis 17:10-14.}}, and exalteth ʿĪsā al-Masīḥ to be Lord Sovereign{{fn:Romans 10:9; 1 Corinthians 8:6; Philippians 2:9-11. In the Qur’an, 3:79-80 and 5:72-75.}}, is no gospel of heaven. If ʿĪsā al-Masīḥ Himself hath said, “I am come not to destroy the law,”{{fn:Matthew 5:17, in the Authorised Version: “Think not that I am come to destroy the law, or the prophets: I am not come to destroy, but to fulfil.”}} how then saith Paul, “Ye are not under the law”?{{fn:Galatians 5:18; compare Romans 6:14 and Galatians 3:25. Christian interpreters read these of the law as a means of justification. See <a href="/paul/paul-against-jesus/">Paul against Jesus</a>.}} Do two voices proceed from one Lord, or hath enmity arisen among them that wrote?',
				'Brother, I say not this to cast thee down, but that thou mayest awake. Hast thou searched out whom thou followest? If ʿĪsā al-Masīḥ never taught godhead for Himself, and yet thou dost worship Him, who then hath turned thy worship aside? Dost thou not tremble, lest thou stand one day before God, Most High and Glorious, and perceive that he whom thou didst follow was one who was never sent, yet styled himself a servant of Christ Jesus?',
				'I write with a flood of tears{{fn:Paul speaks of his own tears in the same way: Acts 20:19, 31; 2 Corinthians 2:4; Philippians 3:18.}}: forsake Paul, and return unto the true teaching of ʿĪsā al-Masīḥ. Follow Him as a servant of God, Most High and Glorious, and not as a Lord Sovereign lifted up by the tongues of men. Worship Him alone who is One{{fn:Deuteronomy 6:4; Mark 12:29. In the Qur’an, 112:1-4.}}, and not him whom words and the devices of men have made a god.',
				'Carry my greeting to all that seek the truth in the true ʿĪsā al-Masīḥ. Hold fast to the oneness of God{{fn:“Hold fast” is Paul’s own phrase for steadfastness (1 Thessalonians 5:21; 2 Thessalonians 2:15).}}, and shun the gospel that came not from heaven, nor from an apostle, nor from ʿĪsā al-Masīḥ, but from one who repeateth the name of Christ Jesus as a shield for his error.',
				'This letter have I written with mine own hand{{fn:Paul closes letters in his own hand as a mark of authenticity (1 Corinthians 16:21; Galatians 6:11; Colossians 4:18; 2 Thessalonians 3:17).}}, as a token that I live no more in the shadow of Paul.',
				'The grace of God, Most High and Glorious, who sent ʿĪsā al-Masīḥ as His servant{{fn:The benediction follows Galatians 6:18 and Philippians 4:23: “The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with you.”}}, and not as a Lord Sovereign held on high, be with thee that seekest the truth. And mayest thou be loosed from that shadow, and return unto the teaching of the true messenger. Amen.',
				'I, who aforetime walked in the shadow of Paul, am now turned unto ʿĪsā al-Masīḥ, peace be upon him, servant of the true Lord Sovereign.',
				'## About this chapter',
				'This chapter is an English rendering, in the manner of the Authorised Version, of the Malay letter <em>Epistel Kepada Jemaat Paulus</em> (“An Epistle unto the Congregation of Paul”). It follows the form of the Pauline letters: salutation, thanksgiving, exhortation, an autograph line, and a benediction. It states the Islamic position and is set apart from the chapters above as a bonus. For the evidence behind its claims, see <a href="/paul/acts-galatians/">Acts against Galatians</a> and <a href="/paul/james/">James and the Jerusalem church</a>.',
			),
		),

		// Resources.
		array( 'path' => 'resources', 'title' => 'Resources', 'template' => $section, 'pattern' => 'section-resources', 'excerpt' => 'Timeline, glossary, source library, FAQs and objections.' ),
		array(
			'path' => 'resources/timeline', 'title' => 'Timeline', 'excerpt' => 'From the ministry of ʿĪsā ibn Maryam to the fixing of the canon.',
			'body' => array(
				'Dates are approximate and follow the mainstream scholarly range. “c.” means circa.',
				'- c. 6-4 BCE: Birth of Jesus, in the reign of Herod the Great.',
				'- c. 27-30 CE: Ministry of Jesus; crucifixion under Pontius Pilate, who was governor from 26 to 36 CE.',
				'- c. 50 CE: 1 Thessalonians, probably the earliest surviving Christian writing.',
				'- c. 50-60 CE: Paul’s main letters: Galatians, Corinthians, Romans.',
				'- 62 CE: James, the brother of Jesus, put to death in Jerusalem.',
				'- c. 70 CE: Mark. Destruction of the Temple by Rome.',
